  "prompt": {
    "file": "drone-prompt-20261117-230546.txt",
    "text": "Design a quadcopter for everyday carry-along aerial photography. Strict orthographic top view, nose toward the top edge of the image, arms fully unfolded.\nDesigner's note: 机身防泼溅，接缝少，机臂到机身的过渡连续。\nFour motors in a True X layout: the four motor centres sit at the corners of a square. Mean opposite-motor (diagonal) span 250 mm; motor-centre width-to-length span ratio W/L 1.00; adjacent arm angles clockwise from the front-left motor: 90°, 90°, 90°, 90°.\nMatch the motor centres and frame links in the attached layout image: red dots mark motor centres, blue lines mark frame links. Do not reproduce the dots or lines in the result.\nEach motor carries one stationary 2-blade propeller, 5-inch (127 mm) diameter. The two blades point in exactly opposite directions from the hub.\nNo propeller guards.\nEach arm is 15 mm wide and 4 mm thick; seen from above it reads as a 15 mm wide beam.\nFuselage design envelope 100 × 80 × 30 mm (length × width × height); this is a size limit, not a shape.\nA forward-facing camera sits at the nose and looks horizontally toward the top edge of the image; no lens glass faces the viewer, and nothing camera-like appears at the tail.\nLow landing skids with 40 mm ground clearance, tucked beneath the aircraft.\nShow the complete product on a plain light background as a photorealistic industrial design rendering. Omit guide marks, dimensions and lettering.",
    "text_sha256": "4c79075704eacaee4f67351863e828e6eb2c7144ed106630515d77b8a6315437",
